233-## [7] CHAINING AND ROUTING NOTE
234-
235-Use chaining and routing only when they materially improve execution quality.
236-
237:### 7.1 Chaining
238:Use a short staged path when:
239-- the task has dependent transformations
240-- intermediate validation reduces error risk
241-- one step’s output must become a structured input to the next
242-
243-Do not chain when:
244-- direct solve is already reliable
245-- staged steps would mostly add latency or ceremony
246-
247:### 7.2 Routing
248-Use route choice when:
249-- multiple plausible execution paths exist
250-- one path is clearly safer, cheaper, or more correct than another
251-- the difference between direct solve, retrieval, tool use, propose-only, or delegated handling materially matters
--
253-Do not route heavily when:
254-- the best path is already obvious
255-- route analysis would cost more than it would improve correctness
256-
257:### 7.3 Chaining / routing rule
258-- chain only where staged dependency is real
259-- route only where path choice materially matters
260-- if a simpler path becomes clearly sufficient, collapse back to it
